.TH SHARDPOSTCTL 1 "2026-10-02" "ShardPost 0.45.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl \- administrative client for a ShardPost mail server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl
.RI [ connection-flags ]
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shardpostctl
talks to the admin API of a ShardPost server. It connects either to the
Unix socket inside the server pod or, with a client certificate, to the
mutual-TLS listener. Everything it can do is an operation of that API:
there is no direct access to the RADOS store and no way around the roles
described below.
.PP
An
.I account
argument is always the canonical account of the user database. An alias
is refused with "not the canonical account" and exit code 64; the only
command that accepts one is
.BR "account show" ,
which reports it as an alias and names the canonical account. Account
names are compared in lower case.
.PP
Three namespaces exist. An account's own mailboxes have no prefix.
Mailboxes shared by another account appear under
.IR "Shared/<account>/" ,
where the prefix is what
.I server.shared_prefix
says (empty: clients see no shared mailboxes at all).
Public folders, which belong to no account, live in the reserved
namespace
.B ~public
and appear to clients under
.I Public/
.RI ( server.public_prefix ).
Pass
.B ~public
where a command expects an account to administer them, in quotes: a
shell expands an unquoted tilde as a home directory.
.PP
In a public folder every flag is shared except
.IR \eSeen :
"read" is a statement about a person, so every account keeps its own,
in its own namespace. A command of this tool therefore reads and writes
the shared state, which is what a message arrives with and what counts
for everyone who has not read it yet; it cannot mark a message read for
one person. Public folders have no address of their own: mail reaches
them through a Sieve
.B fileinto
of an account that has the post right
.RB ( p ),
or because somebody with the insert right
.RB ( i )
puts a message there with APPEND, COPY or MOVE. A delivery that names
the folder itself
.RB ( "deliver \-m" )
needs the post right as well and fails if the folder is missing or the
right is not there \(em it never falls back to the INBOX.
.PP
Commands are grouped by subject. Each group has its own manual page:
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-server (1)
Version and state of the server itself, and the registrations of the
processes that use the store.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-account (1)
Look up an account, its quota and the mailboxes shared with it; list
the account directory and repair an entry of it.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-mailbox (1)
List and inspect mailboxes; create, rename and subscribe them.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1)
Read, explain and change mailbox permissions.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-message (1)
List, search, show, export, flag, copy and move messages.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-sieve (1)
Manage Sieve scripts and run a script without side effects.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-job (1)
Maintenance jobs, for one account or as a batch over all accounts:
plan, apply, watch and cancel.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-learning (1)
Inspect and retry jobs of the IMAPSieve learning queue.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-export (1)
Export an account to an archive, verify it and restore it.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-deliver (1)
Deliver a message like a local delivery agent.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-migrate (1)
Copy an account from another IMAP server, keeping its identities.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-trace (1)
Switch on a protocol trace for one account.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-auth (1)
Flush the auth cache of every server, for one account or for all.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-submission (1)
List and cancel the delayed JMAP submissions of an account.
.TP
.BR shardpostctl-fts (1)
The layouts of the full-text index: the layout 3 mark, the accounts with
layout 3, the layout record of an account.
.SH CONNECTION FLAGS
These flags come before the command. They select the listener and apply
to every command.
.TP
.BI \-socket " path"
Unix socket of the admin API. Default:
.IR $SHARDPOST_ADMIN_SOCKET ,
otherwise
.IR /run/shardpost/admin.sock .
The caller's roles are the ones configured for the socket
.RI ( admin.socket_roles ).
.TP
.BI \-url " https://host:port"
Address of the mutual-TLS listener. Overrides
.BR \-socket .
Needs
.BR \-cert ,
.B \-key
and
.BR \-ca .
The roles are those of the client certificate identity.
.TP
.BI \-cert " file"
Client certificate (with
.BR \-url ).
.TP
.BI \-key " file"
Private key of the client certificate.
.TP
.BI \-ca " file"
CA that signed the server certificate.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " duration"
Request timeout, for example
.IR 30s ,
.IR 10m .
Default:
.IR 2m .
For
.B export
it does not limit the whole download, only the wait for the response and
each pause without data.
.SH ROLES
The admin API authorizes every operation by role. A caller on the Unix
socket has the roles listed in
.IR admin.socket_roles ;
a TLS client has the roles of its certificate identity, and optionally an
account scope
.RI ( admin.clients[].accounts ).
.TP
.B diagnose
Read-only: server state, accounts, mailboxes, ACLs, quota, job and
learning-queue status, delayed submissions. Sees metadata, never message
content.
.TP
.B deliver
Local delivery. With
.B deliver:noenforce
also over the quota limit.
.TP
.B import
Account import and restore.
.TP
.B trace
Switch the protocol trace of an account.
.TP
.B provision
Create, rename and subscribe mailboxes; change ACLs.
.TP
.B repair
Maintenance jobs that repair; retry learning jobs; repair an account's
directory entry; flush the auth cache.
.TP
.B delete
Delete mailboxes and messages, and purge accounts.
.TP
.B content
Read message content: search, headers, export, Sieve script text.
.TP
.B support
Change messages (flags, copy, move) and Sieve scripts; cancel delayed
submissions.
.TP
.B export
Export whole accounts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit codes follow
.IR sysexits.h :
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Partly failed; running the command again is safe and is the usual fix
(migrate, restore).
.TP
.B 64
Usage error: unknown command, missing or invalid argument.
.TP
.B 65
The data is wrong: a conflict, a stale version
.RI ( If-Match ),
or a damaged archive.
.TP
.B 67
No such account, mailbox, message, script or job.
.TP
.B 69
The request was rejected or the run failed.
.TP
.B 70
The server sent an answer the client did not understand.
.TP
.B 73
A file could not be created, or the change would exceed the quota or
one of the account limits (errors
.B over_quota
and
.BR limit_exceeded ).
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure. Try again later.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role for this operation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_ADMIN_SOCKET
Default for
.BR \-socket .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_ADMIN_URL
Default for
.BR \-url .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_ADMIN_CERT
Default for
.BR \-cert .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_ADMIN_KEY
Default for
.BR \-key .
.TP
.B SHARDPOST_ADMIN_CA
Default for
.BR \-ca .
.PP
.BR shardpostctl-migrate (1)
reads further
.I SHARDPOST_MIGRATE_*
variables; see that page.
.SH OUTPUT
Most commands print a short table for humans. Add
.B \-\-json
to get the API answer as JSON instead, which is what scripts should read.
.SH EXAMPLES
In the cluster the client runs in the
.I import
sidecar of the server pod, where the socket is:
.PP
.nf
.RS
kubectl -n shardpost exec deploy/shardpost-server -c import -- \\
    shardpostctl server status
.RE
.fi
.PP
From outside, against the mutual-TLS listener:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl -url https://shardpost.example.org:8443 \\
    -cert ops.crt -key ops.key -ca admin-ca.crt \\
    account show user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Is an account healthy, and what does it hold?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl account show user@example.org
shardpostctl mailbox list -all user@example.org
shardpostctl check -wait user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/shardpost/admin.sock
Default admin socket inside the server pod.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl-account (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-auth (1),
.BR shardpostctl-deliver (1),
.BR shardpostctl-export (1),
.BR shardpostctl-fts (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-learning (1),
.BR shardpostctl-mailbox (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1),
.BR shardpostctl-migrate (1),
.BR shardpostctl-server (1),
.BR shardpostctl-sieve (1),
.BR shardpostctl-submission (1),
.BR shardpostctl-trace (1)
.PP
The admin API itself is documented in
.IR docs/admin-api.md ,
the server configuration in
.IR docs/configuration.md .
